# CODEOWNERS -- foundation-tier module review gate.
#
# Closes the gap docs/internal/18-cicd.md's own branch-protection section
# records twice: a change touching pkgcore/dbkit/tenancy and other
# foundation-tier modules is documented as needing a designated owner's
# approval via CODEOWNERS, and its architecture-discipline table names
# CODEOWNERS (over go/pkgcore and go/tenancy specifically) alongside manual
# review and function-level doc convention as the enforcement mechanism for
# the "system context is callable only by whitelisted modules" rule -- no
# such file existed anywhere in this repository before this one, so
# GitHub's own "Require review from Code Owners" branch protection setting
# had nothing to bind to regardless of whether it was ever turned on.
#
# Foundation tier, per root CLAUDE.md's own dependency-floor framing
# ("pkgcore -> dbkit / observability / ratelimit -> tenancy -> config /
# jobs -> ..."): go/pkgcore is the dependency floor every other module
# sits on, go/dbkit is the very next layer (the dual-dialect *gorm.DB
# wrapper, the mandatory Repository[T], and the WithSystemContext
# escape-hatch primitive dbkit/audit itself must call by its bare string
# rather than an import -- see that package's own module.go doc comment),
# and go/tenancy sits directly above both (the tenant-resolution
# Middleware, the audited WithSystemContext wrapper business code is meant
# to call instead of pkgcore's raw primitive, and the tenancytest isolation
# suites every other module's repositories are required to run). A defect
# in any of the three propagates to every module built on top of it.
#
# PLACEHOLDER TEAM HANDLE: @vislake/foundation-reviewers below does not
# correspond to a real GitHub team in this organization -- there is no real
# team to bind to yet. Replace it with a real team (or a list of real
# individual reviewers) before relying on GitHub's "Require review from
# Code Owners" branch protection setting to actually enforce anything;
# until then, this file documents the INTENDED review gate but GitHub
# cannot request a review from a team that does not exist.
#
# Last match wins (this is GitHub's own CODEOWNERS matching rule, not a
# convention this file invents), so more specific paths are listed after
# broader ones where both could apply.

go/pkgcore/  @vislake/foundation-reviewers
go/dbkit/    @vislake/foundation-reviewers
go/tenancy/  @vislake/foundation-reviewers
